u have to work  really hard  um and you know get some lucky breaks  and be in the right place at the right  time and so on so  um  i think there's a significant um  misperception in in the group which is  how wealthy they are and a lot of this  shows up in surveys you ask people you  know how much do the people at the top  make and they say oh wow you know the  average ceo makes million dollars a year  and of course that's that's preposterous  and the average ceo makes 17 or 18  million dollars a year for a top 500  company so um  i think there's a lot of misperceptions  and the one you mentioned the middle  class delusion is is really big one  can you talk a little bit about the  racial makeup of this group that you're  describing generally speaking  yeah and pretty simply it's mostly white  i mean um  it's about 90 white um as against you  know depends how you measure at 65 to 70  of the general population  um there definitely are  uh people of color uh in in in the 9.9  percent a significant group but in  relative to the representation in the  total population it's significantly  lower so um i think the black shares  about 1.7  of the 9.9 as against about 13 of the  general population latinos roughly  similar  in terms of um political power i mean we  understand the dynamics of money and  political power in the united states  generally speaking  but that top 9.9  is much  bigger in terms of population by  definition than the top 0.1 percent in  terms of aggregate power and that  doesn't mean it's being wielded  effectively but in terms of aggregate  power what is the power dynamic between  that top 0.1 and the following 9.9  yes it's um the 9.9 presents this big  political problem especially for the  democratic party frankly because  um that group now is is leaning  uh blue it's not completely rude but  it's more so than the general population  whereas the top 0.1  uh notwithstanding all the  nonsense of ultimate liberals actually  functionally leans very much to the  right  and  and the dynamic between them it's a  really great question it's  you know on the one hand 9.9 is  fundamentally dependent on the 0.1  percent uh economically but on the other  hand culturally  um  there is a big gap and a significant  part shifting left is aware of that and  tries to pull away the big danger for um  the democratic party and the left  is that  this  group which is i think has a  disproportionate weight in left  politics um  doesn't necessarily understand or share  the interests of the other 90 of the  population  so they they tend to skew  the left in ways that can be um  ultimately  go against the interests of the large  part of the population and sort of work  indirectly for the top oh that's  interesting so let's see if we can think  about that a little so you've got so the  top 0.1 percent is  leans overtly to the republican side  and it's sort of clear that the economic  interests of that group in terms of the  policy they want is not good for the  bottom 90  it sounds like you're saying with this  the following 9.9 percent nominally  these are folks who identify  more as democrats than republicans and  believe themselves to lean more left  than they lean right  but maybe without even really knowing it  the policies they want or the status quo  that they reinforce is also not good for  that bottom ninety percent  yeah i mean a classic example for me is  is the real estate game so the um uh  we don't actually pay a lot of attention  to that this issue in national politics  but it actually drives a lot of our  culture and it drives a lot of the  issues that we have this the the huge  segregation economic and racial  in our neighborhoods and the the role of  the 9.9 and that i would say on  aggregate is really pretty regressive  because what it ends up doing is is  saying oh the issue isn't you know our  neighborhoods the issue is has to do  with um you know any number of other  things you know personal prejudices with  education 