ndirectly  subsidizing a lot of people invisibly so  you know a classic case point is the  mortgage interest deduction  but there are many other bits and pieces  like that that have the effect of um  subsidizing the 9.9 percent  in ways that are kind of invisible so  people in that group  think that their taxes are actually  higher than they are  um another going back to real estate  that's another kind of invisible subsidy  we have um  we not withstanding the fact that we do  have property uh municipal property  taxes we basically don't capture the the  implicit rental value of real estate and  so people who own their own homes end up  getting an indirect tax subsidy but  they're completely unaware of this  they think they're geniuses for having  bought into the real estate market when  they did and so they're they're kind of  unaware that some of the money that's  coming their way is really an indirect  tax subsidy so it's a really complicated  picture and of course you know i'm sure  you'd agree if we could wave magic want  we'd replace the current taxes to have  something radically more progressive  and a lot more fair yeah i've had very  interesting conversations with folks who  say  you know i don't really make that much  and i'm touching the 33 federal tax  bracket and then when i live if i live  in new york or california or  massachusetts then you're throwing five  to five to ten percent on top of that  and i'll often say to these folks listen  you don't have to tell me exactly what  you make but  understand that you're touching that top  33 percent but not all of your income is  taxed at that because we have you know  these are these are brackets uh marginal  tax brackets and then you've got your  standard deduction  um and you have a mortgage and you've  got you know all these different things  you should really look at that first  page of your 1040 which says total tax  divide it by your income and you are  going to find a shockingly low number  and many 